(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-endo2-part1-adrenal-quiz"]={"title":"Endocrine Physiology II — Part 1: Adrenal (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which portion of the adrenal gland produces steroid hormones?","choices":["Capsule","Hilum","Cortex","Medulla"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The adrenal cortex produces the steroid hormones (aldosterone, cortisol, androgens). — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"The adrenal medulla is functionally similar to a modified:","choices":["Pituitary gland","Parasympathetic ganglion","Sympathetic ganglion","Thyroid follicle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The adrenal medulla is a modified sympathetic ganglion (postganglionic sympathetic neurons). — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Which adrenal cortical zone produces aldosterone?","choices":["Zona glomerulosa","Medulla","Zona fasciculata","Zona reticularis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aldosterone is made in the zona glomerulosa (outermost). — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Which adrenal cortical zone produces cortisol?","choices":["Capsule","Zona glomerulosa","Zona fasciculata","Zona reticularis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cortisol is made in the zona fasciculata (middle zone). — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Which adrenal cortical zone primarily produces DHEA?","choices":["Medulla","Zona glomerulosa","Zona reticularis","Zona fasciculata"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. DHEA (androgens) come from the zona reticularis (innermost). — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"All adrenal cortical hormones are synthesized from:","choices":["Tryptophan","Tyrosine","Glycine","Cholesterol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All adrenal cortical hormones derive from cholesterol. — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Adrenal cortical hormones are:","choices":["Catecholamines","Water soluble","Lipid soluble","Peptides"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cortical steroid hormones are lipid-soluble. — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Steroid hormones primarily exert their effects by:","choices":["Activating tyrosine kinase receptors","Binding intracellular receptors and altering gene transcription","Opening calcium channels","Opening sodium channels"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Steroids bind intracellular receptors and alter gene transcription. — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"The principal target of aldosterone is the:","choices":["Collecting duct principal cell","Thick ascending limb","Proximal convoluted tubule","Glomerulus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aldosterone's principal target is the collecting duct principal cell. — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Aldosterone primarily increases:","choices":["Glucose filtration","Potassium reabsorption","Sodium reabsorption","Calcium excretion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aldosterone increases sodium reabsorption. — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Aldosterone causes increased secretion of:","choices":["Calcium","Potassium","Glucose","Sodium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aldosterone increases potassium secretion (and H+ secretion). — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Which acid-base disturbance occurs with excess aldosterone?","choices":["Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Excess aldosterone drives H+ loss → metabolic alkalosis. — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"The two strongest regulators of aldosterone secretion are:","choices":["Epinephrine and insulin","ACTH and cortisol","CRH and ACTH","Angiotensin II and plasma potassium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aldosterone is controlled mainly by angiotensin II and plasma potassium. — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"ACTH has what effect on aldosterone secretion?","choices":["Major regulator","No effect whatsoever","Completely inhibits secretion","Required for secretion but has little effect on secretion rate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ACTH is required for secretion but has little effect on the secretion rate (that's Ang II/K+). — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Chronic aldosterone excess would MOST likely produce:","choices":["Hyperkalemia","Hypotension","Hyponatremia","Hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic aldosterone excess causes sodium/water retention → hypertension. — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Cortisol is produced primarily by the:","choices":["Zona glomerulosa","Zona reticularis","Zona fasciculata","Adrenal medulla"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cortisol comes from the zona fasciculata. — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Approximately what percentage of circulating cortisol is bound to corticosteroid-binding globulin (CBG)?","choices":["10%","90%","50%","30%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. ~90% of cortisol is protein-bound (CBG + albumin). — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"The biologically active form of cortisol is:","choices":["Cortisol sulfate","Free cortisol","Protein-bound cortisol","Cortisone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Only free cortisol is biologically active. — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Cortisol increases blood glucose primarily by:","choices":["Stimulating gluconeogenesis","Increasing insulin secretion","Blocking glucagon","Increasing glucose filtration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cortisol raises glucose mainly by stimulating gluconeogenesis. — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Which tissue experiences increased protein synthesis in response to cortisol?","choices":["Liver","Skin","Skeletal muscle","Bone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cortisol increases hepatic (liver) protein synthesis while catabolizing peripheral protein. — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Cortisol promotes mobilization of:","choices":["Sodium","Calcium","Potassium","Fatty acids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cortisol mobilizes fatty acids (lipolysis). — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Which is NOT an anti-inflammatory action of cortisol?","choices":["Decreased prostaglandins","Increased histamine release","Decreased T-cell activity","Decreased leukotrienes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Increased histamine release is NOT anti-inflammatory — cortisol suppresses histamine. — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"The hypothalamic hormone that stimulates ACTH release is:","choices":["TRH","GHRH","GnRH","CRH"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. CRH stimulates ACTH release. — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"ACTH stimulates cortisol synthesis primarily by:","choices":["Inhibiting pregnenolone formation","Blocking cholesterol uptake","Increasing cholesterol transport into mitochondria","Blocking steroidogenesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. ACTH acts by increasing cholesterol transport into mitochondria (the rate-limiting StAR step). — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Cortisol secretion is normally highest:","choices":["Noon","Midnight","Early morning","Evening"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cortisol peaks in the early morning (diurnal rhythm). — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Licorice toxicity inhibits which enzyme?","choices":["5a-reductase","Tyrosine hydroxylase","Aromatase","11B-HSD2"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Licorice inhibits 11β-HSD2. — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Licorice ingestion causes cortisol to:","choices":["Activate mineralocorticoid receptors","Inhibit ACTH secretion only","Become inactive","Block aldosterone receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With 11β-HSD2 blocked, cortisol activates mineralocorticoid receptors (apparent mineralocorticoid excess). — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Which adrenal medullary hormone is produced in the greatest quantity?","choices":["Epinephrine","Dopamine","Serotonin","Norepinephrine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine is the adrenal medulla's major product (~80%). — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Which amino acid is the precursor of epinephrine?","choices":["Phenylalanine","Tryptophan","Tyrosine","Glycine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Catecholamines (including epinephrine) are synthesized from tyrosine. — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"},{"q":"Integration: A patient presents with hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis. Which hormone is MOST likely elevated?","choices":["Insulin","Glucagon","Aldosterone","Cortisol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypertension + hypokalemia + metabolic alkalosis = aldosterone excess (hyperaldosteronism). — Endocrine Physiology II — Adrenal lecture"}]};
